  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStorms forming in the Midwest today have a low probability of\nmaintaining strength tonight as a low-level jet and nwd-\nadvancing warm front help maintain only modest stabilization in\na moistening boundary layer. If storms are maintained, it will\nbe possible to see a stronger gust or two, but the likelihood of\nimpactful weather tonight remains low.\n\nOf greater concern is potential for stronger low-topped\nconvective development on Fri, where buoyancy concentrated in\nthe lower levels of the atmosphere will overlap favorably with\nconvergence within a pre-frontal trough within a moderately\nsheared environment.\n\nThere remains timing/positional uncertainty in the placement of\nthe pre-frontal trough convergence zone, with some guidance\nsuggesting a slower progression. If this slower progression\nmaterializes, there will be better potential for destabilization\nas enhanced ascent overspreads the region within a strong mid- \nlevel jet streak.\n\nBetter potential for severe weather exists to the east of the\nUpper Ohio Valley, but at this time, we cannot rule out the\npotential for the wrn position of the initiating boundary. If it\nbecomes clear that the boundary is favoring earlier passage, the\nsevere weather threat locally will be minimized.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nCold advection in the wake of a strong cold-frontal passage \nwill support renewal of lake-enhanced showers Fri night within a\nplume of steeper low-level lapse rates. Temperature is not\nexpected to cool sufficiently for notable snow, although low\n(<10%) chances for snow exist at the highest elevations of the \nWV ridges thru the weekend.\n\nElevated wind and cloud cover Fri night are likely to preclude\nfrost/freeze concerns, but conditions will become more favorable\nSat night as wind slackens and cloud cover dissipates.\n\nCluster analysis suggests two potential H5 patterns that will\ngovern the Sat/Sun evolution:\n\n1) The scenario that is more likely suggests the H5 trough\nadvancing bodily ewd, with cold air building in Sat night\nleading to widespread temperature in the 30-35F range and\npotential frost/freeze conditions.\n\n2) A second scenario features low pressure cutting off from the\nmain trough, leading to a quicker departure, less cold air\nintrusion, and cooler temperature/frost potential favoring \nmainly the ridges/northeastern most zones.\n\nEnsemble Sensitivity Analysis (ESA) suggests that the second \nscenario is driven by the strength of a H5 ridge currently \nbuilding in the wrn CONUS, and the timing of phasing of TS Simon\ninto the broader flow. If the ridge is stronger and TS Simon\nslower to phase, it favors a cutoff low development and warmer\noutcome as discussed in Outcome 2 above.\n\nMonitor the forecast for frost/freeze potential. Greatest\nconfidence in impactful temperature will be the northeastern\nzones, as temps of 30-35 are most likely there in either \noutcome above.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nIn the wake of the trough, quite a bit of uncertainty grows with\nthe medium-range pattern next week. Much of the guidance favors\na H5 ridge building across the Midwest, suggesting increasing\nwarmth for the Upper Ohio Valley. However, other guidance\nsuggests troughing could develop, which would favor cooler\ntemperature.\n\nThe degree of ridging or troughing in the east appears highly\ncorrelated to the position of a wrn-CONUS trough/potential\ncutoff low around Mon/Tue timeframe. Given this wide variability\nand dependence on atmospheric flow features that do not yet \nexist, the forecast next week has very low predictability, and \nshould be consumed with that awareness.",
